SUMMARY:AI Agents: Working for You or Working Against You?
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  &#160\;  &#160\;  &#160\;  &#160\;  &#160\;  &#160\;
 ABOUT"Trust\, but Verify: Living with AI Agents"Consumer AI agents promis
 e better deals\, cleaner inboxes\, and relief from the steady enshittific
 ation of apps and platforms. Many expect them to shift power toward buyer
 s\, and sellers seem to agree: Amazon and\, more recently\, United Airlin
 es have pushed back rather than cede any control of their platforms.Benea
 th the promise\, though\, lie hard questions of trust. How do we know our
  agents are finding the best deals\, or that they understand what we want
  when we often don't know ourselves? And how do we know they aren't manip
 ulating us\, whether for the companies that built them or for sellers who
  use prompt injection to turn them into double agents?Drawing on Elinor O
 strom's work on governing shared resources\, this talk asks how buyers\, 
 firms\, and regulators can sustain trust in agent-driven global markets. 
 The answer isn't to stop building agents. Buyers will still need to bewar
 e\, just of different things.Interested in AI\, digital privacy\, consume
 r power\, or the future of technology? Join the conversation!WHEN:Tuesday
 \, December 1st from 4:00-5:30 pm  WHERE:IU Student Building\, Room 150  
 BIOGRAPHYChris Savagehas spent forty years practicing law at the point wh
 ere new technology outruns old rules. From the breakup of the phone monop
 oly through the commercial Internet\, online privacy\, and now artificial
  intelligence. He is a partner in the Technology\, Communications\, Priva
 cy and Security group at Davis Wright Tremaine\, and teaches AI Law\, Cyb
 erlaw\, and Information Privacy Law at Catholic University's Columbus Sch
 ool of Law. A Harvard Law School graduate\, he is the author of "Managing
  the Ambient Trust Commons" (Stanford Technology Law Review)\, which draw
 s on Elinor Ostrom's work to treat online privacy as a common-pool resour
 ce problem. His current research asks what happens to contracts\, commerc
 e\, and trust when parties to a deal are AI agents. Meeting URL: https://
 iu.zoom.us/meeting/register/ECvwJiP6RJKH4R_BIB7-qg
