SUMMARY:VIRTUAL EVENT: Can We Trust AI? Inside the Government's Approach 
 to Implementing Responsible AI for National Security
DESCRIPTION:AI is transforming how government organizations access inform
 ation\, analyze data\, and make decisions. But successfully adopting AI\,
  particularly in national security\, requires more than technology.  Draw
 ing on his experience supporting the Department of War's War Data Platfor
 m\, David Kalishman will explore how government organizations are putting
  responsible AI governance into practice through three foundational pilla
 rs:Policy: Establishing standards\, governance frameworks\, and accountab
 ility.    Process: Turning governance principles into repeatable practice
 s that enable secure and scalable AI adoption.    People: Building the le
 adership\, workforce capabilities\, and organizational culture needed to 
 sustain adoption and trust.      The session will also introduce the publ
 icly available CDAO Responsible AI Toolkit as a real-world example of ope
 rationalizing responsible AI principles. To make the session interactive\
 , students will participate in a short hands-on exercise evaluating a hyp
 othetical government AI use case\, identifying risks\, and recommending s
 afeguards before deployment.Key Takeaways for Students:AI Governance &amp
 \; National Security: Balancing innovation\, security\, accountability\, 
 and operational effectiveness.    Beyond Technology: Understanding how Po
 licy\, Process\, and People enable responsible AI adoption at scale.    I
 nterdisciplinary Leadership: How professionals in public policy\, interna
 tional affairs\, and technology can shape the future of AI governance.   
  Format: 45-minute presentation followed by a 15-minute Q&amp\;A.Speaker 
 BioDavid Kalishman is a Senior Manager at Accenture\, supporting the Depa
 rtment of War's War Data Platform\, a major initiative focused on advanci
 ng enterprise data accessibility\, artificial intelligence\, and data-dri
 ven decision-making across the defense enterprise.  His work focuses on s
 trengthening Data and AI Governance through three interconnected pillars:
  Policy\, Process\, and People. This includes establishing governance str
 uctures\, operational processes\, and organizational capabilities that ac
 celerate AI adoption while maintaining security\, accountability\, and re
 sponsible oversight. Working at the intersection of technology\, strategy
 \, and national security\, David focuses on translating emerging AI capab
 ilities into practical and safe implementation approaches. He is also an 
 alumnus of the Hacking for Defense (H4D) community. Meeting URL: https://
 iu.zoom.us/my/hlspesp
