LOCATION:Hamilton Lugar School
SUMMARY:CLACS Faculty Research Symposium with Luciana Namorato
DESCRIPTION:The Center for Latin American and Caribbean Studies (CLACS) w
 elcomes Dr. Luciana Namorato to present\, When a Reflex Becomes the Key: 
 What Women Artists Do with the Ways They Are Read\, as&#160\;part of the 
 Fall 2026 CLACS Faculty Research Symposium.Thursday\, December 3rd\, 4-5P
 M at the Hamilton Lugar School (355 N Eagleson Ave.) GA1060Abstract:  Why
  do we so often explain a woman artist's work by her life? Editing two bo
 oks on Latin American women artists\, I saw this become a reflex. For Lat
 in American women\, two more reflexes follow: they are read through label
 s\, and their work ends up classed as "craft." My research asks how women
  artists have answered these reflexes\, often turning them into opportuni
 ties\, as Frida Kahlo did with her life story. What shuts doors as a refl
 ex may open them as a key. This talk turns to two Brazilian artists\, the
  writer Amara Moira and the poet and dancer Gal Freire\, for whom the lab
 els accumulate ("woman\," "Latin American woman\," "transgender"). By wor
 king through the reflexes that confine how they are read\, Moira and Frei
 re make their work a fruitful space for questions that concern every arti
 st: life and work\, the particular and the universal\, art and craft.Bio:
 Luciana Namorato\, Associate Professor in the Department of Spanish and P
 ortuguese at IUB\, is co-editor of The Other Fridas: The Lives and Works 
 of Latin American Women Artists (2025) and Da margem à palavra: Escritor
 as brasileiras do Oitocentos à atualidade (2026). Her research takes a t
 ransatlantic approach to Luso-Brazilian and Latin American literatures\, 
 with particular attention to the relationships among literature\, gender\
 , and ethnic and national identities. She holds a Ph.D. from the Universi
 ty of North Carolina at Chapel Hill and\, as a Fulbright fellow\, conduct
 ed postdoctoral research at the University of Coimbra\, Portugal.  &#160\
 ;
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p data-pm-slice="1 1 []">  The Center for L
 atin American and Caribbean Studies (CLACS) welcomes Dr. <strong>Luciana 
 Namorato</strong> to present\, <em><strong>When a Reflex Becomes the Key:
  What Women Artists Do with the Ways They Are Read\,</strong></em> as&#16
 0\;part of the Fall 2026 CLACS Faculty Research Symposium.</p><p data-pm-
 slice="1 1 []"><strong>Thursday\, December 3rd</strong>\, <strong>4-5PM</
 strong> at the <strong>Hamilton Lugar School</strong> (355 N Eagleson Ave
 .) <strong>GA1060</strong></p><p><strong>Abstract:</strong></p><p>  Why d
 o we so often explain a woman artist's work by her life? Editing two book
 s on Latin American women artists\, I saw this become a reflex. For Latin
  American women\, two more reflexes follow: they are read through labels\
 , and their work ends up classed as "craft." My research asks how women a
 rtists have answered these reflexes\, often turning them into opportuniti
 es\, as Frida Kahlo did with her life story. What shuts doors as a reflex
  may open them as a key. This talk turns to two Brazilian artists\, the w
 riter <strong>Amara Moira</strong> and the poet and dancer <strong>Gal Fr
 eire</strong>\, for whom the labels accumulate ("woman\," "Latin American
  woman\," "transgender"). By working through the reflexes that confine ho
 w they are read\, Moira and Freire make their work a fruitful space for q
 uestions that concern every artist: life and work\, the particular and th
 e universal\, art and craft.</p><p><span style="font-style: inherit\; fon
 t-weight: inherit\;"><strong>Bio:</strong></span></p><p><span style="font
 -style: inherit\; font-weight: inherit\;"><strong>Luciana Namorato</stron
 g>\, Associate Professor in the Department of Spanish and Portuguese at I
 UB\, is co-editor of <em>T<strong>he Other Fridas: The Lives and Works of
  Latin American Women Artists</strong></em> (2025) and <strong><em>Da mar
 gem à palavra: Escritoras brasileiras do Oitocentos à atualidade</em></
 strong> (2026). Her research takes a transatlantic approach to Luso-Brazi
 lian and Latin American literatures\, with particular attention to the re
 lationships among literature\, gender\, and ethnic and national identitie
 s. She holds a Ph.D. from the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill
  and\, as a Fulbright fellow\, conducted postdoctoral research at the Uni
 versity of Coimbra\, Portugal.</span></p><p>  &#160\;</p><p><picture clas
