LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
SUMMARY:ACES Horizon Talk: "Spring Fever in the Gobi: Labor\, Economy\, a
 nd Care in Cashmere Harvest Interactions" by Dr. Kathryn Graber
DESCRIPTION:The Association of Central Eurasian Students (ACES) warmly in
 vites you to our upcoming Horizon Talk\, featuring Dr. Kathryn Graber. Jo
 in us for an engaging discussion titled "Spring Fever in the Gobi: Labor\
 , Economy\, and Care in Cashmere Harvest Interactions."  &#160\;  In Mong
 olia's rural herding economy\, it is difficult to overstate the importanc
 e of the spring cashmere harvest. For most herding families\, cashmere is
  the main source of cash income for the year. So before their goats begin
  to naturally shed\, herders quickly comb them\, capturing the valuable u
 ndercoat from millions of animals nation-wide over a few feverish weeks. 
  Combing sessions are about more than extraction\, however. This talk exa
 mines human-goat and human-human interactions during combing sessions in 
 the Gobi to show what else is achieved in these moments: sharing labor\, 
 aligning perception\, working out property relations\, and caring for bot
 h the animals and each other.  &#160\;  Sponsored by: Central Eurasian St
 udies (CEUS) and the Inner Asian and Uralic National Resource Center (IAU
 NRC).  &#160\;  Please note that this event will be in person only. There
  will not be a Zoom or hybrid option. Lunch will be provided.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody">  The 
 Association of Central Eurasian Students (ACES) warmly invites you to our
  upcoming Horizon Talk\, featuring <strong>Dr. Kathryn Graber</strong>. J
 oin us for an engaging discussion titled <strong>"Spring Fever in the Gob
 i: Labor\, Economy\, and Care in Cashmere Harvest Interactions."</strong>
 </p><p>  &#160\;</p><p data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody">  In Mongolia's
  rural herding economy\, it is difficult to overstate the importance of t
 he spring cashmere harvest. For most herding families\, cashmere is the m
 ain source of cash income for the year. So before their goats begin to na
 turally shed\, herders quickly comb them\, capturing the valuable underco
 at from millions of animals nation-wide over a few feverish weeks.</p><p>
   Combing sessions are about more than extraction\, however. This talk ex
 amines human-goat and human-human interactions during combing sessions in
  the Gobi to show what else is achieved in these moments: sharing labor\,
  aligning perception\, working out property relations\, and caring for bo
 th the animals and each other.</p><p>  &#160\;</p><p>  Sponsored by: Cent
 ral Eurasian Studies (CEUS) and the Inner Asian and Uralic National Resou
 rce Center (IAUNRC).</p><p>  &#160\;</p><p>  Please note that this event 
 will be in person only. There will not be a Zoom or hybrid option. Lunch 
 will be provided.</p>
